To include the CWD (Change Working Directory) and PASV (Passive Mode) commands in the sequence of client requests, we can place them in logical positions. Typically, you would want to enter PASV mode before sending a LIST command, and you might want to change the working directory (CWD) before listing its contents or creating a new directory (MKD).

Here’s the modified sequence of client requests, including the CWD and PASV commands:

```
USER ubuntu
PASS ubuntu
SYST
PWD
CWD /path/to/directory  # Specify the directory you want to change to
PASV                     # Switch to passive mode
PORT 127,0,0,1,132,209   # This command is typically not needed in PASV mode
LIST
MKD test
QUIT
```

In this example, replace `/path/to/directory` with the actual directory you want to change to. Note that the PORT command is not typically used after entering PASV mode, as the server will provide the client with the necessary information for the data connection. Depending on how you want to structure the commands, you may choose to omit the PORT command entirely. 

Here’s an alternative modified sequence without the PORT command, assuming you are using PASV mode for the data transfer:

```
USER ubuntu
PASS ubuntu
SYST
PWD
CWD /path/to/directory  # Specify the directory you want to change to
PASV                     # Switch to passive mode
LIST
MKD test
QUIT
```

This version makes more sense for a typical FTP session when using PASV

